From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: allocate tx status packet IDs cyclically

mt76_tx_status_skb_add() took the lowest free packet ID. When the status
of a frame timed out (MT_TX_STATUS_SKB_TIMEOUT), its ID was given to the
next tracked frame of the station at once, and a TXS that arrived late
for the old frame was matched to the new one. On the XR500v this showed
up with a station that kept the hardware queue busy: hostapd logged
"authenticated" for Authentication responses that the client never
processed, because their status was a timeout turned into an ACK
(MT_DRV_IGNORE_TXS_FAILED) or a stale TXS.

Use idr_alloc_cyclic() over the same range, so an ID is reused only
after the other ~120 have been handed out.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/tx.c b/tx.c
index 1af0f37..6e27650 100644
--- a/tx.c
+++ b/tx.c
@@ -159,8 +159,13 @@ mt76_tx_status_skb_add(struct mt76_dev *dev, struct mt76_wcid *wcid,
 		goto out;
 	}
 
-	pid = idr_alloc(&wcid->pktid, skb, MT_PACKET_ID_FIRST,
-			MT_PACKET_ID_MASK, GFP_ATOMIC);
+	/*
+	 * Hand out packet IDs cyclically: with the lowest free ID, the ID of
+	 * a frame whose status timed out was reused right away, and a late
+	 * TXS for the old frame was then credited to the new one.
+	 */
+	pid = idr_alloc_cyclic(&wcid->pktid, skb, MT_PACKET_ID_FIRST,
+			       MT_PACKET_ID_MASK, GFP_ATOMIC);
 	if (pid < 0) {
 		txs_alloc_fail++;
 		pid = MT_PACKET_ID_NO_SKB;
